############ SOURCE-LEVEL DECOMPOSITION (forward_sweep_warp, full-distribution launch) ############
Built out-of-tree with -lineinfo; register usage IDENTICAL to committed build (56/40/91), so codegen
unchanged.  Per-SASS-instruction "Avg. Threads Executed" from ncu --section SourceCounters
(raw rows in ncu_sass_fwd.csv).  Instructions inside `if (lane == 0)` always execute with exactly
1 active lane, so this decomposition needs no modelling assumption.

558 SASS instructions   warp-inst 764,394,140,836   thread-inst 3,460,648,794,587
sum-derived avg active lanes = 4.53   (whole-kernel metric reported 4.52 -> decomposition is complete)

              avg active lanes         warp-inst   % issue       thread-inst   % work
 EXACTLY 1 (lane-0 serial)      584,462,238,493     76.5%   665,361,073,919    19.2%
                    >1-4         55,968,644,614      7.3%   240,866,038,811     7.0%
                    >4-8          3,052,661,784      0.4%    19,713,086,505     0.6%
                   >8-16         28,556,335,909      3.7%   290,613,167,459     8.4%
                  >16-24         62,149,296,987      8.1% 1,277,536,610,325    36.9%
                 >24-<32                     0      0.0%                 0     0.0%
      EXACTLY 32 (full warp)     30,204,963,049      4.0%   966,558,817,568    27.9%

VERDICT: 76.5% of instruction ISSUE runs on a single lane, producing only 19.2% of the useful work.
The efficient phase-1 band work (16-32 lanes) is 12.1% of issue but 64.8% of the work.
The top single-lane instructions by issue count (19.7e9 executions each) decode to phase 2's
k-descending trim scan -- LDG.E.U8 (Cbuf[idx], unsigned char) + IMAD.WIDE x4 (curV[idx], int) both
indexed off `idx = k - curbase`.  This CONFIRMS the earlier 77-85% estimate (actual 76.5%) and
REFUTES the competing hypothesis that the low SIMT efficiency came from phase-1 slide divergence:
phase-1 work concentrates in the 16-32 lane buckets, not the low ones.
